{"id":"s3.6.1","slug":"limits","title":"Limits","content":"<p class=\"mb-4\">Most limit problems on the test can be solved by direct substitution. However, if this results in an indeterminate form like $\\frac{0}{0}$, you must try other methods.</p><p class=\"mb-4\"><strong>Factoring:</strong> If you have a rational function, try factoring the numerator and denominator to cancel common terms. For example, $\\lim_{x\\to2} \\frac{(x-2)(x+3)}{(x-2)(x+5)} = \\lim_{x\\to2} \\frac{x+3}{x+5} = \\frac{5}{7}$.</p><p class=\"mb-4\"><strong>L'Hôpital's Rule:</strong> For indeterminate forms $\\frac{0}{0}$ or $\\frac{\\infty}{\\infty}$, you can take the derivative of the numerator and denominator separately and then evaluate the limit. For example, $\\lim_{x\\to0} \\frac{\\sin x}{x} = \\lim_{x\\to0} \\frac{\\cos x}{1} = 1$.</p>","summary":"Most limit problems on the test can be solved by direct substitution.","generation_guideline":"Generate a limit problem that can be solved by substitution, factoring, or L'Hôpital's Rule.","questions":[{"id":1,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac{3x+8}{7x-4}$.","answer":"3/7","kind":"exact","tags":["fractions","calculus"]},{"id":2,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to4} (2x-6)$.","answer":"2","kind":"exact","tags":["calculus"]},{"id":3,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to2} \\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}$.","answer":"4","kind":"exact","tags":["fractions","calculus"]},{"id":4,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to2} \\frac{x^3-8}{x^2-4}$.","answer":"3","kind":"exact","tags":["fractions","calculus"]},{"id":5,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to\\infty} \\frac{3x-1}{x}$.","answer":"3","kind":"exact","tags":["fractions","calculus"]},{"id":6,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to3} \\frac{x^3-27}{x-3}$.","answer":"27","kind":"exact","tags":["fractions","calculus"]},{"id":7,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to0} \\frac{x^2-3x}{x}$.","answer":"-3","kind":"exact","tags":["fractions","calculus"]},{"id":8,"text":"Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to3} \\frac{x^3-27}{x^2-9}$.","answer":"4.5","kind":"ex